# Auth & RBAC

Authentication is Better Auth with the **admin plugin** + **access control**. The auth instance is a per-request factory (no module singleton) because D1 is only reachable through a request-bound binding.

## Building auth
`packages/auth/src/auth.ts` exports `createAuth(db: Database, cookiePlugin?: BetterAuthPlugin, runInBackground?: (task: Promise<unknown>) => void)` — it wires the Drizzle adapter (`provider: 'sqlite'`), the `admin({ ac, roles, defaultRole: DEFAULT_ROLE, adminRoles: [ADMIN_ROLE] })` plugin and the two plugins that guard it (`guardAdministrators(db)` and `refuseOwnRoleChange`, see "Assigning roles"), then appends the cookie plugin it receives. Without one, session refresh is disabled. `runInBackground` becomes `advanced.backgroundTasks.handler`: `getAuth()` passes the Worker's `waitUntil`, so a link's delivery does not delay the answer.

- **Server Components / Route Handlers (web):** never import a singleton. Use the seam `getAuth()` from `@/lib/server`, then `const auth = await getAuth()`. `getAuth()` resolves the request's D1 binding and calls `createAuth(db, nextCookies(), runInBackground)`, with the Worker's `waitUntil` as `runInBackground`.
- **tRPC procedures:** the context already injects `ctx.db` and `ctx.session`. To check a permission, use `permissionProcedure` (below), never `userHasPermission`: that plugin endpoint knows the compiled roles only, not the roles of the database. When a procedure needs Better Auth itself, build it from the request db: `createAuth(ctx.db)`. That instance has no cookie plugin, so tRPC reads never extend the session; only call endpoints through it that set no cookie on the caller, as the `users` router's admin calls do (`impersonateUser`, for one, sets a cookie and belongs in the app).

## Roles & permissions
Two kinds of permission, with a hard boundary between them:

- **Account administration** (`user`, `session`: list, create, ban, set-role, impersonate...) belongs to the Better Auth `admin` plugin. It is compiled in `packages/auth/src/permissions.ts` (`accountStatement`, from the plugin's `defaultStatements`) and only the `admin` role holds it. It is never stored and no role created at runtime can gain it. `admin` is `adminRoles` in `auth.ts`; the plugin checks it itself.
- **Business resources** (`document` today) are the vocabulary of `businessStatement` in the same file: the only resources and actions a role can be given, and the only source of valid names. What each role may do on them is **data**, read from the database on every request:
  - `roles` has one row per role: `id` is the value of `users.role`, plus a `label`.
  - `role_permissions` has one row per permission: `role_id`, `resource`, `action`, unique on the three, deleted with the role.

`admin` has no rows. `permissionsOf` (`packages/auth/src/role-permissions.ts`) gives it everything it is compiled with, every business action included, without reading the database. Any other role holds exactly the rows it has: a role missing from `roles`, a role with no rows, an empty database all hold nothing, so a partial setup refuses. A row is honored only when it names `businessStatement`, so a `user.delete` row written by hand grants nothing.

`permissionsOf` reads what a role holds and `isGranted` tests a permission against that list; `hasPermission(db, role, resource, action)` is the two together, for a caller outside tRPC (the upload route). They keep nothing between calls, so a permission removed from `role_permissions` is refused on the next request. Inside tRPC `callerPermissions` (`packages/api/src/middleware/rbac.ts`) reads the list once for a whole request (every procedure of a batch or of a page's prefetches shares it, keyed by the request's `Headers` in a `WeakMap`, so it dies with the request), and `permissionProcedure` hands it to the procedures below it as `ctx.permissions`.

The compiled `roles` of `permissions.ts` exist because the `admin` plugin rejects a role name it does not know when it assigns one. Only `admin` holds permissions there; `user` and `manager` are accepted names with none. A role you create in the database does not need to be compiled: it is assigned through `users.setRole`, which `guardAdministrators` carries out itself. Every Better Auth upgrade should re-check this boundary: the plugin still has no dynamic access control, which is why the roles live in application tables.

### Default roles
No migration is shipped, so the tables start empty. `{{runScript "db:roles"}}` (local) and `{{runScript "db:roles:remote"}}` (production) write the roles of `DEFAULT_ROLES` in `packages/auth/src/default-roles.ts` (`user`: create, read, update and delete its own documents, `manager`: read every user's documents with `document.read-any`, and delete none). `{{runScript "local-setup"}}` runs the local one, and the deploy steps run the remote one after the migrations. The defaults are written once, into a database that has no role: once any role exists the command writes nothing, so a default role you deleted, a permission you revoked and an emptied matrix are never written back, and running it again changes nothing. If a run was interrupted half way (roles with no permission), delete the rows of `roles` and run it again. Every definition is validated against `businessStatement` before anything is written (`defaultRoleStatements`), so a default can never name `user` or `session` or the reserved `admin`.

### Editing roles
The **Roles** page (`/admin/roles`) edits the matrix at runtime, with no deployment: tick a box to grant a permission, clear it to revoke it, create a role, delete one. Every change goes through the `roles` tRPC router (`packages/api/src/router/roles.ts`: `matrix`, `create`, `setPermission`, `remove`), which calls the rules of `packages/auth/src/role-management.ts`.
- **Only the literal `admin` role manages roles**, through `adminProcedure`, never through a permission a role could be granted: a role that could edit the matrix could give itself everything, so there is no subset check to get wrong. `user,admin`, `Admin` and a role that holds every business permission are refused like any other role.
- **A grant names the code vocabulary.** `setPermission` takes `resource.action` and accepts a name of `businessStatement` only; `user.delete`, `session.revoke` and an unknown name are a 400 and write nothing. Granting a permission a role already holds, or revoking one it does not, changes nothing.
- **`admin` is neither edited nor deleted**, and `admin`, the default role (`DEFAULT_ROLE`, the role every new account starts on) and every key every object has (`constructor`) are reserved: no role is created under any of these ids. The plugin looks a role up by plain object key, so an account on `constructor` would get a 500 from its endpoints; no path accepts the id, a role assigned or created. The default role can be edited, but not deleted, because accounts are created on it with no check that it exists.
- **A role id** matches `^[a-z0-9-]+$` and has at most 40 characters, its label 1 to 64. A duplicate id is refused (the primary key decides, so two requests for one id make one role). There is no rename of an id: it is stored on each account, and renaming it would leave every holder on a role that no longer exists. Create the new role, give its holders the new role, delete the old one.
- **A held role cannot be deleted, in the write itself.** D1 has no interactive transaction, so a read ("does anyone hold it?") followed by a delete would let an account be given the role in between. `deleteRole` is one conditional statement, `DELETE FROM roles WHERE id = ? AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM users WHERE role = ?)`, sent with its permission rows in one `db.batch` (D1 runs a batch as one transaction; the rows go only once the role is gone, so a refused delete keeps them). Assigning a role is the mirror (`assignRole` writes only while the role has its row), so the two cannot interleave into an account that holds a deleted role. A banned account still holds its role. The grant is conditional the same way, so a role deleted a moment earlier gets no permission row that a role created later under the same id would inherit.
- **A change applies on the next request.** Nothing keeps a permission list between requests (see above), so a revoked box is refused for everyone who holds the role on their next call, and the page does not guess: a box changes only after the server confirmed it and the matrix was fetched again. A box that is saving is `aria-busy` and ignores clicks (it is never `disabled`, which would drop the keyboard focus), and a polite status region says `Saving`, `Saved` or `Not saved: <reason>` and when a click was ignored, so a screen reader hears the result.
- **Refusals are worded as answers** (`No role has that id`, `A role with that id already exists`), never with `does not exist`, `table` or `column`: Better Auth logs a thrown message with one of those at error level, as a database failure.

## Assigning roles
A role is assigned to a user in one way: exactly one id that exists. The id matches `^[a-z0-9-]+$` (`ROLE_ID`) and is either `admin` or a row of `roles`. The `admin` plugin is looser than that: it splits `user.role` on a comma and reads `user,admin` as an administrator (impersonation, `/admin/*`), accepts an array of roles and stores it as `a,b`, and checks a role against the compiled `roles` by key (`constructor` passes, and the account that holds it gets a 500 from the plugin's endpoints), while `permissionsOf` reads `user,admin` as no role at all. Every path that writes a role therefore goes through `packages/auth/src/guard-administrators.ts`, a plugin in front of the five endpoints that write a role, a ban or a removal, so a tRPC procedure, `auth.api.*` and a direct request to `/api/auth/admin/*` meet the same rules:

- **The caller is exactly `admin`.** A caller whose role the plugin merely reads as administrator (`user,admin`, a role stored before this guard existed) is refused on all five, and an anonymous request too (`401`). The one anonymous call that goes through is `/admin/create-user` from the server with no request and no headers (a script), which the role rule still applies to.
- **`/admin/set-role`** assigns any role of the database, a compiled one or not, and answers like the plugin (`{ user }`). The role must be one string: an array, a comma list, `Admin`, ` admin`, `constructor` or an id with no row is a `400`. A user cannot change their own role (`You cannot change your own role`).
- **`/admin/create-user`** accepts `admin` or the default role (`DEFAULT_ROLE`) in `role` or `data.role`, and nothing else: the plugin writes the account after this guard has read, so a role that can be deleted (`manager`, or any role you create) could be deleted in between and leave an account on a role nobody has. Create the account and give it the role with `set-role`, whose write is conditional on the role being there. `users.create` does exactly that: it creates the account with the default role (`DEFAULT_ROLE`) and then assigns the chosen one with `setRole`, so if that second step fails the account stays on the default role, the lowest privilege, never a higher one. The toast shows the refusal as it was worded and the users list is refreshed, as the account exists.
- **`/admin/update-user`** writes no `role`, `banned`, `banReason` or `banExpires` (a `400` that names `set-role` or `ban-user`): the other fields still update.
- **The last administrator** is never demoted, banned or removed. An administrator that is banned does not count. The check is part of the write, not a read before it: D1 has no interactive transaction, so `packages/auth/src/role-assignment.ts` writes with one conditional statement (`UPDATE users SET role = ? WHERE id = ? AND (role <> 'admin' OR EXISTS (another administrator who can sign in))`, the same `WHERE` on the ban and on `DELETE`) and reads the rows it returned. Two administrators who demote, ban or remove each other at once cannot both succeed. `set-role` and `remove-user` run that statement in the guard and skip the plugin's endpoint (it cannot assign a role it does not know, and it cannot find a row it just deleted); `ban-user` marks the ban with that statement first and ends the sessions, and the plugin completes it (reason, expiry). A ban or a removal of yourself is refused before anything is written.

Account administration stays the plugin's. The guard depends on how the installed plugin treats these paths: the comma list, the array, the lookup of a role by key, the absence of a last-administrator rule, the list of its endpoints and how a `before` hook ends a request. Re-check them on every Better Auth upgrade, and guard any endpoint the plugin adds that writes a role, a ban or a removal.

### What `admin` can do
`user.role = admin` is equivalent to root, and the guard does not change that. An administrator can:

- **impersonate** any user who is not an administrator (`/admin/impersonate-user`) and act as them, their data and their permissions included;
- **set any user's password** (`/admin/set-user-password`, the `users.setPassword` procedure) and then sign in as them, an administrator's included;
- **change any user's email** (`/admin/update-user` with `email`) and then use the password reset flow to take the account over;
- list and revoke any user's sessions, create users, and ban or remove any account but the last administrator.

Hand out `admin` as you would a production credential. The roles you create in the database never gain any of this: a stored permission can name business resources only.

## Enforcing permissions
Server-side (tRPC) — `packages/api/src/middleware/rbac.ts`:

- `adminProcedure` — rejects unless `ctx.session.user.role` is `admin`: account administration.
- `permissionProcedure(resource, action)` — tests the list of `callerPermissions` and throws `FORBIDDEN` on a refusal. Only business resources and their actions are accepted, so a misspelled name or `permissionProcedure('user', 'ban')` fails type checking (account administration is `adminProcedure`'s). The documents router gates `list` and `count` with `('document', 'read')` and `delete` with `('document', 'delete')`, and takes the scope of the rows from the list too (`packages/api/src/document-scope.ts`): `document.read-any` shows every user's documents, `document.delete-any` deletes anyone's, and a role without them works on its own rows, whatever the role is called.
- `permissions.mine` (`packages/api/src/router/permissions.ts`) returns the flat list of the caller (`['document.read', ...]`), the same list `callerPermissions` read for the request.

Client-side — `apps/web/src/components/can.tsx` + `apps/web/src/hooks/use-permission.ts`:

- The dashboard layout awaits `permissions.mine` on the server and hydrates it into the query cache, so the sidebar, the command palette and every `<Can>` are right on the first render.
- `usePermission(permissions)` returns a verdict: `unknown` (the list is not in the cache: nothing renders), `granted` (every listed action is held) or `denied`. `usePermissions()` is the raw list and `verdictOf(list, request)` the pure check. The list is refetched with the usual TanStack Query rules, so the interface follows the matrix; the server refuses regardless.
- `useAllowedRoutes()` of `src/lib/allowed-routes.ts` filters `ROUTES` on a granted verdict. The sidebar (`sidebar-links.tsx`) and the command palette (`command-palette.tsx`) both list pages through it, so a page the permission list does not grant appears in neither, and the sidebar hides a whole category label when none of its links are granted. It takes the role of the session the shared components pass and ignores it: a role is only a name here, what it may do is in the database. The Documents route asks for `document.read`, so a role without it has no link, and the documents page (`page.tsx`) answers it with a no-access message instead of starting its queries.
{{{{raw}}}}
- `<Can permissions={{ document: ['create'] }}>…</Can>` renders its children only on a granted verdict, its `fallback` on a denied one and nothing while unknown. Example from the documents page: the upload form is wrapped in `<Can permissions={{ document: ['create'] }}>` and each delete button in `<Can permissions={{ document: ['delete'] }}>`.
{{{{/raw}}}}

`authClient` (`packages/auth/src/auth-client.ts`) is built with `adminClient({ ac, roles })` for the plugin's own client calls. Do not use its `checkRolePermission` for a business resource: it reads the compiled roles, not the database.

## Adding a permission
1. Add the action (or a new resource with its actions) to `businessStatement` in `permissions.ts`. `Permission`, `PermissionRequest` and `permissionProcedure` are typed from it, and `admin` holds it at once, with no further change.
2. Grant it to the roles that need it: tick its box in the **Roles** page of a running project. For a fresh project, also add it to the entries of `DEFAULT_ROLES`: the defaults are written only into a database with no role, so an existing database is edited in the page.
3. Gate server endpoints with `permissionProcedure('<resource>', '<action>')` and UI with `<Can>`. Never add a stored row that names `user` or `session`: it is ignored.

To add a role, use **New role** on the Roles page, then tick its permissions. The role selects of the create-user dialog and the user editor list `admin` and the rows of `roles` (`users.assignableRoles`), so the role can be assigned at once, with no change to `permissions.ts`.

## Searching records
The command palette finds records through the `search.records` procedure (`packages/api/src/router/search.ts`, `protectedProcedure`), which returns `{ type, label, items: { id, title, subtitle, href }[] }[]`; the palette knows no type, so it shows whatever groups come back. `SEARCHABLE_TYPES` in `packages/api/src/search.ts` is the one list of what can be found (documents and users). An entry declares `canSearch` (the permission, tested against `caller.permissions`, the list of `callerPermissions` that `permissionProcedure` reads, or the admin role for users), `find` (the query, at most the `limit` it is given) and the `href` of each item (a record without a detail page links to its list page). A type the caller may not read gets no group, and no query runs for it. Documents are scoped as `documents.list` scopes them (`visibleDocuments` in `packages/api/src/document-scope.ts`): a role holding `document.read-any` finds every document, any other role holding `document.read` its own. A document's subtitle is its size and upload date, written with the helpers of the documents list.
- The text is cut at `MAX_SEARCH_LENGTH` when the input is read (`packages/api/src/search-query.ts`, which also holds `SEARCH_MIN_LENGTH` for the palette), trimmed, and nothing is searched under 2 characters; each type returns at most `SEARCH_LIMIT_PER_TYPE` (5) items. The match is `containsText` (`packages/api/src/text-match.ts`, the rule of the users list search): plain text, so `%` and `_` match themselves.
- The match scans the table: `instr(lower(column), lower(text))` cannot use an index, and an index on the column does not change that. Each query reads its rows in the order of an index and stops at its limit, which is accepted at this size. Beyond it, the path is a full-text index (SQLite FTS5) or a prefix match (`LIKE 'text%'` on a column with an index, which no longer finds text in the middle of a title).
- To make a new type searchable, add one entry to `SEARCHABLE_TYPES`; the palette needs no change.

## Post-login redirect
The login form reads the destination from the `redirectTo` query parameter and passes it through `safeRedirectPath` (`apps/web/src/lib/safe-redirect.ts`) before `router.push`: only an internal path (a single leading `/`, same origin) is kept, anything else falls back to `/`. Never hand a query parameter straight to `router.push` or `redirect`: an absolute URL would send a freshly signed-in user to another site.

## Password reset
`emailAndPassword.sendResetPassword` (in `auth.ts`) hands the reset link to `deliverLink({ kind: 'reset-password', ... })` in `packages/auth/src/link-delivery.ts`. The blueprint has no mailer, so that function logs the link to the server console (`[reset-password] <email> → <link>`), in development and in production. In production it also logs `No mailer is configured`: the link is only in the server logs (the Worker logs), so a user who forgot a password cannot reset it from their inbox. `NODE_ENV` is `production` in a deployed Worker (a wrangler var). Before going live, plug a mail provider (Resend, SES, Postmark, ...) into `deliverLink`, the one place every link leaves the server (the password reset link, the email change link, and the notice to the old address after a change). The UI is `apps/web/src/app/(auth)/forgot-password` (requests a link via `authClient.requestPasswordReset({ email, redirectTo: '/reset-password' })`) and `reset-password` (reads the `?token` query and calls `authClient.resetPassword`).

